Stop od -A reading past the end of its radix tables.
The tables giving the address radix letter and padding for each -A
argument had entries for d, o and x but not n, so -A n read both from
past the end of the arrays. The padding read is used to indent the
lines after the first of each block when there are several formats,
so with -A n those lines were indented by an arbitrary amount instead
of not at all. An empty -A argument matched the terminating NUL of the
list of radix letters and read further still, garbling the address
format. Add the entries for n, with no padding, and reject an empty
argument, quoting the whole argument in the message rather than its
first character, which for an empty argument is a NUL.

---
 coreutils/od_bloaty.c |    8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/coreutils/od_bloaty.c
+++ b/coreutils/od_bloaty.c
@@ -1240,17 +1240,17 @@
 	if (opt & OPT_A) {
 		static const char doxn[] ALIGN1 = "doxn";
 		static const char doxn_address_base_char[] ALIGN1 = {
-			'u', 'o', 'x', /* '?' fourth one is not important */
+			'u', 'o', 'x', 'o' /* fourth one is not important */
 		};
 		static const uint8_t doxn_address_pad_len_char[] ALIGN1 = {
-			'7', '7', '6', /* '?' */
+			'7', '7', '6', '0' /* no address, no padding */
 		};
 		const char *p;
 		int pos;
 		p = strchr(doxn, str_A[0]);
-		if (!p)
+		if (!p || !str_A[0])
 			bb_error_msg_and_die("bad output address radix "
-				"'%c' (must be [doxn])", str_A[0]);
+				"'%s' (must be [doxn])", str_A);
 		pos = p - doxn;
 		if (pos == 3) G.format_address = format_address_none;
 		address_base_char = doxn_address_base_char[pos];
